jarvis-agent on Debian and Ubuntu
=================================

Start
  systemctl --user start jarvisd   (it also starts by itself at every login)
  jarvis setup                     choose a model provider: local Ollama, LAN or cloud
  jarvis                           chat; jarvis ask "..." for a single request

Approving actions
  Jarvis asks before it changes anything. The terminal shows a numbered
  list: type a number to tick or untick an item, a to approve the ticked
  items, d to deny. Passwords are asked for without echo, never in chat.

Installing and removing packages, restarting services
  Approved actions run through jarvis-helper (system D-Bus, as root),
  guarded by polkit. jarvisd runs as a user service, outside your login
  session, so polkit only lets members of the group jarvis-admins use the
  helper:

    sudo adduser "$USER" jarvis-admins     (then log out and back in)

  Only add accounts you would also give sudo: a member can
  install and remove packages and restart allowlisted services through
  Jarvis, after approving the card, without typing a password. Without the group,
  approved package and service actions are refused.

Provider keys
  Cloud API keys are kept in your login keyring (Secret Service). Without a
  running keyring (for example over SSH without a desktop session) use a
  local or LAN provider.

Removing
  Installing jarvis-agent also adds the Jarvis APT repository
  (/etc/apt/sources.list.d/jarvis.sources, "Enabled: no" until the
  repository is published) through jarvis-archive-keyring;
  purging that package removes the source and its signing key.
  sudo apt purge jarvis-agent jarvis-cli jarvisd jarvis-pkg jarvis-diag jarvis-helper jarvis-archive-keyring
